Meniscal Tear
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Joint line tenderness, Delayed effusion after twisting injury, Mechanical locking or catching

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-032
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe knee pain with sudden mechanical locking, rendering the patient completely unable to straighten the leg or bear weight.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient who sustained a twisting knee injury, subsequently developing a rigid, locked knee and severe pain preventing any ambulation. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to evaluate for a displaced bucket-handle meniscal tear or acute osteochondral fracture, and to provide immediate mechanical stabilization and potent analgesia to prevent further intra-articular damage prior to surgical or daytime orthopedic review.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My knee twisted, I felt a horrible pop, and now it's completely jammed; I can't straighten it out and the pain is excruciating when I try."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with acute, severe right knee pain following a mechanical injury 24 hours ago. Timeline: Patient experienced a sudden onset of sharp knee pain associated with a twisting injury while the foot was planted firmly on the ground. Reports a sensation of a distinct pop and tearing feeling at the precise moment of injury. Over the subsequent 24 hours, a moderate joint effusion developed. Tonight, the patient experienced a severe mechanical event: the knee suddenly "locked" during ambulation and remains stuck in 30 degrees of flexion. Any attempt to fully extend the knee causes agonizing, sharp pain.
Functional Status: Acutely immobilized; completely unable to weight-bear or achieve a straight leg.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe fall risk; entirely dependent on spouse for transfers to the bathroom.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 142/88 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Alert, in severe distress, holding the right knee strictly in partial flexion.
Physical Exam: Right knee demonstrates a moderate, tense joint effusion. Exquisite point tenderness is localized directly along the medial tibiofemoral joint line. The knee is mechanically locked, lacking the terminal 30 degrees of extension; forced passive extension encounters a firm, intensely painful rubbery block. Maximal passive knee flexion also provokes severe localized pain. McMurray test is deferred due to the acutely locked status and extreme guarding. Apley grind test produces sharp pain during axial compression and rotation of the flexed knee. Ligamentous testing (Lachman, Varus/Valgus) is stable, indicating intact cruciate and collateral ligaments.
Environmental Observations: Patient restricted to the ground floor; unable to navigate stairs due to the locked limb.
Medication Reconciliation: Took Paracetamol and Ibuprofen with zero relief of the mechanical locking pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Medial Meniscal Tear with Mechanical Locking (Suspected Bucket-Handle Tear) - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Anterior Cruciate Ligament (ACL) Rupture - Ruled out clinically by the firm endpoint on Lachman testing and the delayed, progressive nature of the effusion (rather than an immediate, massive hemarthrosis).

Patellar Dislocation - Ruled out by central tracking and normal anatomical position of the patella, and absence of medial retinacular tenderness.

Loose Osteochondral Defect - Cannot be definitively ruled out without MRI, but the specific joint line tenderness and classic twisting mechanism heavily favor a primary meniscal tear.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a classic history of a twisting injury resulting in a meniscal tear, complicated by an acute, severe mechanical locking event. The inability to fully extend the knee (a locked knee) strongly suggests a displaced meniscal fragment physically blocking the joint hinge. Delaying clinical evaluation risked unmanaged agonizing pain, further structural crushing of the displaced meniscal tissue, and severe fall risk. Urgent after-hours assessment safely confirmed the mechanical nature of the block, ruled out gross ligamentous instability, and facilitated the immediate application of a rigid splint to prevent further painful movement, alongside potent analgesia and an urgent orthopedic referral pathway.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Intramuscular Ketorolac 30mg STAT to reduce acute intra-articular inflammation and capsular pain. Prescribed Codeine 30mg QID PRN for 3 days to manage breakthrough pain.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Wear the provided rigid extension splint continuously, locking the knee in its most comfortable position. Consequence: Prevention of micro-movements that crush the trapped cartilage fragment, immediately halting the sharp, agonizing pain spikes (negative reinforcement).
Action: Strictly use crutches to maintain a non-weight-bearing status. Consequence: Complete offloading of the joint space, allowing the severe throbbing ache and swelling to gradually subside over the next 24 hours (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): Fitted a rigid Zimmer splint. Provided axillary crutches.
Follow-up: Urgent orthopedic surgery referral for MRI and likely arthroscopic surgical intervention to unlock the joint and repair/resect the torn meniscus.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the calf becomes severely swollen, tight, and painful, if the foot turns cold and pale, or if there is a sudden onset of high fever with a red, hot knee.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis of a displaced meniscal tear is clinical and highly suspected based on the locked joint; definitive confirmation requires an MRI. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The knee is mechanically jammed by a piece of torn cartilage. Medications will dull the pain, but the knee will remain locked until it is evaluated and likely treated by an orthopedic surgeon.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if your calf muscle suddenly swells up and becomes painful, or if your toes become completely numb and cold.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A piece of the shock-absorbing cartilage in your knee has torn and flipped into the hinge mechanism, physically jamming it. Trying to force it straight will only crush the cartilage further. The splint and crutches are mandatory to freeze the joint and protect the tissue while we get you to a surgeon.
Actionable Steps: Absolutely no weight-bearing. Do not try to forcefully straighten the leg. Keep the rigid splint on at all times to prevent accidental movement that will trigger severe pain.

